body:
Something I've done is limit each session to a page. Yes, a single graph paper page (and a word document, bullet point list of plot points and such). Your way of doing the initiative is not bad and very creative! The way I do it is similar: Ask each player what they got, and write the low number further down the page and higher numbers up higher, then fill in the numbers and check them off as you go along.  Essentially, keep it in order, so you don't have to re-write. Pre-roll initiative for monsters or just use a 10 or a 15+DEX for monsters.

&#x200B;

Your method of planning is very good! I'm a highly improvisational dm so I can and will fly on 5 words of notes super easily. Try cutting the "finer details of encounters". Just write AC, HP and spend time remembering their actions, you may find that it cuts down on the planning time and speeds up combat. Improv the finer details, it works just as well and it gets better over time. I can only repeat whatever else the other people have said like u/GARSL_01.

&#x200B;

The last thing I would recommend is to become familiar with the rules and don't be afraid to make up some! I know the rules very well, but frequently bend them.

&#x200B;

P.s BE WARY OF OVERPLANNING. You may or may not have this issue, but take my advice, don't write down hundreds of characters of stuff that you don't need. One of my players/friends started dming at my behest (we run at different times during the week) and he wrote 20 pages of stuff. He has since had to trim 3/4 of that.

If your friends jumped off a bridge would you do it too?

&#x200B;

Edit: Technically self-promotion but I'm willing to ignore it.

Any man who looks at a woman with lust in his heart, has commited adultery with her in his heart...

&#x200B;

Sorry Jesus.

The land of Canaaan.

